{"id":"neet-2026r-q056","pn":56,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Chemical Kinetics","st":"Arrhenius equation and plot","q":"For an elementary chemical reaction, the Arrhenius plot is given below.\nIf the energy of activation is 6.64 kJ mol⁻¹ and R = 8.3 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹, the temperature at which the rate constant becomes e² min⁻¹, is","o":["150 K","200 K","250 K","125 K"],"c":1,"s":"ln k = ln A – Ea/RT.\nThe graph shows ln k = 6 at the intercept (1/T = 0), so ln A = 6.\nFor k = e², ln k = 2.\n2 = 6 – (6.64×10³)/(8.3 × T).\n(6.64×10³)/(8.3 × T) = 4.\nT = (6.64×10³)/(8.3 × 4) = 200 K.","fig":{"w":300,"h":204,"alt":"Arrhenius plot: ln k on the vertical axis (ticks 2, 4, 6, 8) against 1/T; a straight line falls from ln k = 6 on the vertical axis to about ln k = 1 at the right.","d":[["l",60,170,60,8],["l",60,170,280,170],["l",56,152,60,152],["l",56,134,60,134],["t",52,138,"2","e"],["l",56,116,60,116],["l",56,98,60,98],["t",52,102,"4","e"],["l",56,80,60,80],["l",56,62,60,62],["t",52,66,"6","e"],["l",56,44,60,44],["l",56,26,60,26],["t",52,30,"8","e"],["l",60,62,250,150.2,"B"],["l",22,125,22,71,"a"],["t",30,54.8,"lnk","s"],["l",120,192,148,192,"a"],["t",154,196,"1/T"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2026-q056","pn":56,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Chemical Kinetics","st":"Units of rate constant","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList-I (Order of reaction):\nA. Zero order\nB. First order\nC. Second order\nD. Third order\nList-II (Unit of rate constant):\nI. mol⁻¹ L s⁻¹\nII. mol⁻² L² s⁻¹\nIII. s⁻¹\nIV. mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III"],"c":2,"s":"The unit of the rate constant of an nth order reaction is (mol/L)^(1–n) s⁻¹.\nZero order, n = 0: unit is mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹.\nFirst order, n = 1: unit is s⁻¹.\nSecond order, n = 2: unit is mol⁻¹ L s⁻¹.\nThird order, n = 3: unit is mol⁻² L² s⁻¹.\n(The publisher wrote n = 2 for the third-order line, but the unit given is the n = 3 one.)\nNote: Printed option 3 reads D-I, but third order matches II (mol⁻² L² s⁻¹); printed answer [3] kept (A-IV, B-III, C-I are right, D looks like a typo)"},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q067","pn":67,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Chemical Kinetics","st":"Zero order reaction","q":"2A → B (rate constant k) is a zero-order reaction, where k = 1.0 mol L⁻¹ min⁻¹. If the initial concentration of A is 2 M, then the time taken to complete 75% of the reaction will be","o":["0.75 min","1.0 min","2.0 min","1.5 min"],"c":0,"s":"For the zero order reaction, −(1/2)(dA/dt) = k.\nt = (A₀ − Aₜ)/2k.\n75% complete leaves Aₜ = 0.5 M.\nt = (2 − 0.5)/2 = 0.75 min."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q083","pn":83,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Chemical Kinetics","st":"Zero order reaction graph","q":"For a certain reaction R → Product, the plot of concentration [R] vs time has a negative slope as shown. The order of reaction is :","o":["0","1","2","2.5"],"c":0,"s":"For a zero order reaction, [R] = [R₀] – kt.\nSo a straight line with a negative slope (k = –slope) shows a zero order reaction.","fig":{"w":260,"h":170,"alt":"Graph of [R] concentration against time: a straight line falling from [R0] on the vertical axis, labelled k = –slope.","d":[["l",90,150,90,12,"a"],["l",90,150,255,150,"a"],["t",98,22,"[R₀]",""],["l",90,30,215,110,""],["t",165,66,"k = –slope",""],["t",84,76,"[R]","e"],["t",84,90,"concentration","es"],["l",140,162,170,162,"a"],["t",176,166,"time",""]]},"sfig":{"w":260,"h":170,"alt":"Graph of [R] concentration against time: a straight line falling from [R0] on the vertical axis, labelled k = –slope.","d":[["l",90,150,90,12,"a"],["l",90,150,255,150,"a"],["t",98,22,"[R₀]",""],["l",90,30,215,110,""],["t",165,66,"k = –slope",""],["t",84,76,"[R]","e"],["t",84,90,"concentration","es"],["l",140,162,170,162,"a"],["t",176,166,"time",""]]}},
{"id":"neet-2026-q088","pn":88,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Chemical Kinetics","st":"Arrhenius equation","q":"Given below is an expression for the rate constant of a first-order reaction occurring at a certain temperature, T (K).\nln k = 14.34 – (1.25 × 10⁴)/T\nThe energy of activation in kcal mol⁻¹ for the reaction is :\n(Given: k in s⁻¹, R = 1.987 cal mol⁻¹ K⁻¹)","o":["12.42","14.34","18.63","24.84"],"c":3,"s":"Arrhenius equation: k = A e^(–Ea/RT).\nln k = ln A – Ea/(RT)\nComparing with the given expression, Ea/R = 1.25 × 10⁴.\nEa = 1.25 × 10⁴ × 1.987 cal/mol\nEa = 24.84 kcal mol⁻¹"},
{"id":"neet-2025-q056","pn":56,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Chemical Kinetics","st":"First order half-life","q":"If the half-life (t₁/₂) for a first order reaction is 1 minute, then the time required for 99.9% completion of the reaction is closest to :","o":["2 minutes","4 minutes","5 minutes","10 minutes"],"c":3,"s":"For a 1st order reaction: kt = 2.303 log (A₀/Aₜ), where A₀ is the initial concentration and Aₜ is the final concentration.\nt₉₉.₉% = 10 t₁/₂.\nt₉₉.₉% = 10 × 1 minute = 10 minutes."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q089","pn":89,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Chemical Kinetics","st":"First order rate constant and time","q":"If the rate constant of a reaction is 0.03 s⁻¹, how much time does it take for 7.2 mol L⁻¹ concentration of the reactant to get reduced to 0.9 mol L⁻¹?\n(Given: log 2 = 0.301)","o":["69.3 s","23.1 s","210 s","21.0 s"],"c":0,"s":"k = 0.03 s⁻¹\nt = (2.303)/(k) log (a)/(a − x)\nt = (2.303)/(0.03) log (7.2)/(0.9)\nt = (2.303)/(0.03) log 8\nt = (2.303)/(0.03) × 3 × log 2\nt = (2.303)/(0.03) × 3 × 0.301\nt = 69.3 s"}
